  "question": "Background / Context:\nThe project involves developing a Feature Flag package specifically designed for the Laravel framework. Feature flags enable developers to introduce new functionality without disrupting the existing codebase. This package allows for the creation of feature groups and assigns users to these groups, providing flexibility in enabling or disabling features for different user cohorts. Additionally, it includes Timebombs to manage technical debt by signaling the need to remove obsolete flags.\n\nObjectives:\n1. Provide an easy-to-use system for managing feature flags within Laravel applications.\n2. Enable grouping of features and assignment of users to these groups, including override capabilities.\n3. Incorporate a mechanism to alert developers about outdated flags to reduce technical debt.\n\nStakeholders & Users:\n- Developers: Primary users responsible for implementing and managing feature flags using the package.\n- CI/CD Managers: Utilize the feature flags to ensure smooth continuous integration and deployment processes.\n- Project Managers: Oversee the project's progress and ensure alignment with business objectives.\n\nFunctional Descriptions:\n1. Feature Management:\n   - Create, read, update, and delete features.\n   - Assign features to specific user groups or individual users.\n   - Activate or deactivate features as required.\n\n2. Feature Group Management:\n   - Create, read, update, and delete feature groups.\n   - Assign users to feature groups for collective feature access management.\n\n3. User Management:\n   - Assign and override specific features for individual users.\n   - Manage user memberships within multiple feature groups.\n\n4. Timebomb Management:\n   - Set expiry dates for feature flags.\n   - Trigger exceptions when a flag reaches expiry, indicating necessary removal from the codebase.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions:\n1. Usability: The system should be intuitive and seamless for developers, integrating well within the existing Laravel environment.\n2. Efficiency: Operations such as activation and deactivation of features should occur without significant delay.\n3. Scalability: Capable of managing large numbers of features and user assignments without degradation in performance.\n4. Maintainability: Documentation should be comprehensive to facilitate easy understanding and use by developers.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0084\nCREATE TABLE `feature_groups` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `feature_groups_name_unique` (`name`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `features` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `expires_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `features_name_unique` (`name`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `users_email_unique` (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature_group_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `feature_group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `feature_group_user_feature_group_id_user_id_unique` (`feature_group_id`,`user_id`),\n  KEY `feature_group_user_user_id_index` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_group_user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_group_id`) REFERENCES `feature_groups` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_group_user_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature_group_feature` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `feature_group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `feature_group_feature_feature_group_id_feature_id_unique` (`feature_group_id`,`feature_id`),\n  KEY `feature_group_feature_feature_id_index` (`feature_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_group_feature_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_group_id`) REFERENCES `feature_groups` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_group_feature_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `features` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature_user_override` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `is_enabled` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `feature_user_override_feature_id_user_id_unique` (`feature_id`,`user_id`),\n  KEY `feature_user_override_user_id_index` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_user_override_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `features` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_user_override_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
